WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.810
MARLEIGH KERR: I'm Marleigh
Kerr, a sophomore studying

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.660
religion and politics,
and it's my honor

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.310
to introduce tonight's speaker.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.880
Mr. Robert R. Reilly
is the director

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.340
of the Westminster Institute.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.090
He was educated at Georgetown
University, the National Cheng

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.860
Shiu University in Taiwan, and
the Claremont Graduate school.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.940
A former US Army tank platoon
leader and former president

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.360
of the Intercollegiate
Studies Institute,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.560
Mr. Reilly served as a special
assistant to President Reagan

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.310
and later as director
of the Voice of America.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.310
He worked in the George
W. Bush administration

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.220
as senior advisor for
information strategy

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.460
under the Secretary of
Defense, and he participated

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.760
in Operation Iraqi
Freedom as senior advisor

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.940
to the Iraqi Ministry
of Information.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.030
He was taught at the
National Defense University

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.370
and recently served
as director of Voice

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.280
of America for a second time.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.310
He is the author of The
Closing of the Muslim Mind--

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.310
How Intellectual Suicide
Created the Modern Islamic

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.400
Crisis, and America on Trial--

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.530
A Defense of the Founding.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.885
Please join me in welcoming
tonight's speaker, Mr. Robert

00:01:20.885 --> 00:01:21.385
R. Reilly.

00:01:21.385 --> 00:01:24.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.220
ROBERT R. REILLY: Thank
you very much, Marleigh,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.020
for that wonderful introduction.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:46.940
Well, I want to thank Doug
Jeffrey and Matt Bell,

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.190
Kim Allison for all she did in--

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.173
sorry, Ellsworth in all the work
she did on the arrangements.

00:01:53.173 --> 00:01:54.590
And most of all,
of course, I want

00:01:54.590 --> 00:02:03.500
to thank Larry Arnn, my former
classmate at Claremont Graduate

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.400
University.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:10.699
He stipulated that my appearance
here was only on condition

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:15.740
that I not tell any stories
from graduate school when

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:23.600
this kid from Arkansas arrived
with a little hay in his hair,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.630
so I'll keep that promise,
Larry, wherever you are.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.780
By the way, I was very pleased
that Marleigh mentioned

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.380
the book, America on Trial--

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:42.740
A Defense of the Founding,
prefaced by Larry Arnn.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.250
Larry told me at
dinner how irritated

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.760
he was that some people have
come up to him and said,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.430
you know Bob Reilly?

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.360
And he says, no,
Bob Reilly knows me.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.180
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.820
So well, here we go.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.860
I'm afraid this is a
very serious subject that

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:14.090
requires some ponderous
thought, and I

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:20.270
was trying on the flight up
here today from Washington, DC,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.170
to try to cut out some things.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.760
But if I go for too long
and cut into the Q&amp;A period,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.320
Matt has promised that he'll
get the hook and keep me quiet.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.970
So our topic is faith,
reason, and education.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.740
I will concentrate on the
relationship between faith

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.060
and reason, which one would
need a very good education

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.840
to explore and to
understand properly.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.310
The very differentiation
of faith and reason--

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:53.090
there's something of a
relatively recent provenance

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.900
and is uniquely Western.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.270
Ancient tribal
man would not have

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.580
understood the distinction.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:05.810
It was born of the contact
between Greek philosophy

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.770
and Christianity.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:13.150
Only that encounter or
collision, if you will,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.970
generated the concepts
in faith and reason

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.850
as two distinct realms.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.550
Tertullian famously
asked, "What has

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:26.230
Athens to do with Jerusalem?"

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.920
This question has reverberated
down the centuries.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.090
Nearer to our own time, the
great political philosopher,

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.890
Leo Strauss, said that these
two cities, the one representing

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.460
philosophy and the
other divine revelation,

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.910
each made their own
comprehensive claim

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.550
to understanding what
completes man according

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.010
to what is highest in him.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:57.490
He suggested that these claims
are antithetical to each other,

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.640
but that neither
can be disproven.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.430
Moreover, he said that
the unresolved tension

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.270
between these two
cities was fructifying.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.310
It is what animated
Western civilization

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.160
and made its great
achievements possible.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.060
Eric Voegelin thought that
the unresolved tension was

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.170
not between these two
cities, but between imminence

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:25.920
and transcendence, and
that reason was just

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:31.460
as much a form of revelation
as revelation itself.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.100
These are two possible answers
to Tertullian's question.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.310
There is not sufficient time
to discuss them in relation

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.150
to Islam or Judaism, but
let's consider these questions

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.230
in regard to Christianity.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.730
Are faith and reason
headed forever

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.410
for a head-on collision
with each other?

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.590
Or are they actually in
separate but parallel lanes

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.770
headed in the same direction
toward a final destination

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.350
in the highest truth?

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:06.060
Do they have nothing
to say to each other,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.130
or can each be understood
better in light of the other?

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.640
Do those who say faith has
nothing to do with reason

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.200
ignore what faith
itself says of reason?

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.920
And is not reason
ineluctably drawn to judge

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.700
whether the content of
faith itself is reasonable?

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.290
Does it have the
competence to do that?

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.260
These are the issues I want
to touch upon this evening.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.540
There's been a lot
of ink and some blood

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.060
spilled over these questions.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.000
Henry Cardinal Manning
in the 19th century

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.640
said, quote, "All human conflict
is ultimately theological,"

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.710
unquote.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.140
Does this mean that people
with different theologies

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.690
must kill each other?

00:06:54.690 --> 00:07:00.090
For extended periods of
history, the answer was yes.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:04.200
My god will defeat your
god on the field of battle.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.140
It was often thought that no
society or political order

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:12.630
could cohere unless it was
religiously homogeneous.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:16.380
This is sometimes referred to
as the theological-political

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.330
problem.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.860
That it need not be so has been
demonstrated by the achievement

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.930
of the United States.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.300
In any case, the answers
to these questions

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.120
will affect the
conduct of our lives

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.130
because they concern
the nature of reality,

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:38.330
our ways of knowing it, and
the truth of things, including

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:39.590
the purpose of our lives.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.800
Of course, the
answers will depend

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.080
on the content of
what reason reveals

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.750
and on what
revelation discloses.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:53.370
I will touch upon the respective
contributions of these cities

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:57.480
and suggest in what way
they illuminate each other.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.900
I'll also try to sketch out the
consequences of the position

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:06.640
that they are completely
bifurcated and antagonistic.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.940
How is the authority of reason
established in the first place?

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.720
Upon what grounds did
Athens claim that the world

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.290
is intelligible to our reason?

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:22.820
What makes it apprehensible?

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:26.900
How could Greek philosophy
claim the aptitude

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.810
to know the answers
to these things?

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.700
From where did its
confidence come

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:35.750
that reason is a
better guide to reality

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.000
than, say, the
divination of animal

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.590
entrails by the high priest?

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:45.610
Some claim it was
Heraclitus who first grasped

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:49.840
that the universe is
an intelligible whole

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.240
and that therefore man is
able to apprehend its order.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.340
How can man do this, and in what
terms can it be articulated?

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:03.690
Heraclitus said the
universe is intelligible

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:09.710
because it is ruled by and
is the product of thought

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:15.170
or wisdom, giving it an
intrinsic rationality.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.550
As far as we know,
Heraclitus and Parmenides

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:25.130
were the first to use the word
"logos" to name this wisdom.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.790
Logos means reason
or word in Greek.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:31.190
Heraclitus meant that
all things are animated

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:33.530
by a rational principle.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.470
He stated that,
quote, "All things

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:42.170
come to pass in accordance
with this logos," unquote.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:46.080
If this is true,
then reason's inquiry

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.950
into the nature of
reality becomes possible.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:55.390
The very idea of nature
becomes possible.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.420
Heraclitus said, quote,
"It is the highest virtue

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:03.340
and true wisdom in speaking
and acting to obey nature

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:06.100
that is the common logos.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.400
Therefore, all human
laws are nourished

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:13.180
by this original
divine law," unquote.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.760
It is logos that makes
the world intelligible

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.680
to the endeavor of philosophy,
and it is why laws, in turn,

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.260
must be reasonable.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.950
As Socrates later
said, quote, "The world

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:31.400
is the product of a mind
which sets everything in order

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.760
and produces each individual
thing in the way that

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:37.400
is best for it," unquote.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:41.340
We can have thoughts
about things that

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.700
are the product of thought.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.900
We can also have thoughts
about the thinker,

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:55.230
leading Aristotle to describe
God as thought thinking itself.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.820
The maker is reflected
in the things

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:02.580
he made since they are not made
accidentally, but according

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:06.300
to logos.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:12.600
Through reason, said Socrates,
man can know what is.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.840
Now, Aristotle said that
what is operates according

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.090
to the laws of nature.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.810
He taught that the essence or
nature of a thing is what makes

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:29.890
it what it is and why it is not
and cannot be something else.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:33.520
In the Politics, he said
that the nature of things

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.860
consists in their
end or consummation.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.720
I'm quoting from him now.

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.630
"For what each thing is when
its growth is completed,

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:45.160
we call the nature
of that thing,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.510
whether it be a man or a
horse or a family," unquote.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:54.340
For example, an acorn
develops into an oak tree.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.150
There is no point along
its trajectory of growth

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.920
that it will turn into
something other than an oak.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.620
That is because it has the
nature of an oak tree and not

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.570
of anything else.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:09.590
It is inwardly directed
to be an oak tree.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:11.360
Hence, by nature
and natural law,

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.670
Aristotle meant the
principle of development,

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:16.400
which makes any
living thing what

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.620
it is and, given the
proper conditions,

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.770
what it will become when
it reaches its perfection

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.510
or fulfillment.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:29.180
For Aristotle, quote, "Nature
ever seeks an end," unquote.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:32.300
He declared, quote, "Nature
does nothing without purpose

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.640
or uselessly," unquote.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:41.060
This end state is its telos,
the reason for which it is.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:45.980
The telos of the acorn is
a fully mature oak tree.

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.560
Philosopher Robert
Sokolowski said, quote,

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:52.670
"The end of a thing
is its perfection.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.320
The nature is what is perfected.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:02.970
The nature and end of a thing
are normative for that thing.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:08.010
The end in particular is how
the thing should be," unquote.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:09.960
The natural law
for each thing is

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:15.630
what allows us to speak
of what it ought to be.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:18.630
This means that what
is good for a thing are

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.060
those things or
actions that assist it

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.370
in reaching its perfection.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.430
For example, the right
kind of soil and moisture

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.130
are good for the
acorn in reaching

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.560
its perfection as an oak tree.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.560
Likewise, those things
that inhibit or prevent

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.380
something from reaching
its end are bad for it

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:42.900
as drought or poisoned soil
would be bad for an acorn.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.910
In each case,
Aristotle would refer

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:48.420
to the good things for
the growth of the oak tree

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:53.550
as natural to it and the
bad things for its growth

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.310
as unnatural to it.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.990
What is good or
natural over something

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:02.200
is therefore intrinsic
to that thing,

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:06.160
internal to, and
inseparable from it.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:12.370
It is not imposed from the
outside, nor can it be altered.

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:13.870
Well, what is the
standard we should

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:17.210
use to measure things
regarding man's nature and end?

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:23.320
And how do we discern what is
in accord with it or against it?

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.530
Aristotle states,
open quotes, "In order

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:30.250
to find what is natural, we must
look among those things which,

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.760
according to nature, are
in a sound condition,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.740
not among those that
are corrupt," unquote.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:40.660
If you want to know
what a healthy man is,

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.930
you don't look at a sick man.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.050
You look at a healthy man.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:46.780
In Politics, he says, quote,
"Thus, the human being

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.730
to be studied is
the one whose state

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.850
is best both in body and soul.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.230
In him, this is clear," unquote.

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:58.690
Thus, because we know what a
human being is in the fullest,

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:01.390
we can understand
what a privation is,

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:04.870
including for each
and every part of man.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.420
For example, let's say,
for the sake of argument,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:10.900
that 20/20 vision is
the best for the eye

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.900
and blindness is the worst.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:17.990
With 20/20 vision, the eye has
reached its complete actuality.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:21.570
It is perfect as an eye.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:24.810
It possesses no potential
to see better than it does.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:26.730
In each case of
imperfection, there's

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:31.080
something missing that
ought to be there.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.910
For instance, the
limb ought to be

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:34.980
able to move in
its full strength.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.370
The ear ought to be able
to hear and the eye to see.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:41.370
The further a thing is
from its perfection,

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:46.050
the more defective or corrupt,
as Aristotle used the term--

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.580
the more corrupt it
is, just as blindness

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:55.400
is the furthest
defect of an eye.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:02.300
A privation of the good
cannot itself be good.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:04.100
In fact, as Saint
Augustine said,

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:07.980
evil is a privation of the good.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:12.390
Now, man alone has the ability
to choose between those acts

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:16.290
or things which are conducive
to his end and those

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.120
which are not.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.480
Animals, plants, or
rocks cannot do this.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.770
Only man can act in defiance
of his nature, which

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.950
is what defines evil for him.

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:32.500
Since man freely
chooses his behavior,

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:37.780
he is the only one for
whom natural law is moral.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:39.880
This is why Aristotle
said, quote,

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:46.090
"The moral activities are
human par excellence," unquote.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:49.600
Therefore, references to
natural law in regard to man

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.210
mean not so much the
physical laws or instinct

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:56.020
to which he's subject like
the lower orders of being,

00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:01.560
but the moral law which
applies exclusively to him.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:07.220
Man does not get to fact
fabricate his end or telos.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:09.710
Human nature is a given.

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:14.390
Its meaning is not located
in man's will or desires,

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:20.150
but in pre-existing
reality, in what is.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:24.650
Consequently, while man can know
what is good or evil according

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:28.640
to his nature, he does
not have the prerogative

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:32.180
to determine what
is good or evil.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:37.040
Oughtness is already in
the given nature of things.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:37.540
Excuse me.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.690
Therefore, man is
morally obliged

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:49.790
to choose the good that will
bring about what ought to be.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:53.030
Otherwise, he will become
less than fully human

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:56.570
and what he ought not to
be, perhaps something worse

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:58.595
than a beast, as
Aristotle warned.

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.120
If everything human must
be measured against it,

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.135
what is man's telos?

00:18:08.135 --> 00:18:13.600
Aristotle answered, quote,
"Happiness is the highest good,

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:19.060
being a realization and perfect
practice of virtue," unquote.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.490
Having established
the primacy of reason

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:26.140
as the highest human faculty,
Aristotle asked in what way

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.450
it served man's end.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:32.690
He answered that the highest
exercise of man's reason

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:36.065
is contemplation of the
highest good, which is divine.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:45.670
He wrote, quote, "Contemplation
is the highest form of activity

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:49.420
since the intellect is
the highest thing in us,

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:52.870
and the objects it apprehends
are the highest things

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:56.260
that can be known," unquote.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:59.950
He said that, quote, "The
activity of God, which

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:03.220
surpasses all others
in blessedness,

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:06.860
must be contemplative,
and of human activities,

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:12.330
therefore, that which
is most akin to this

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:17.280
must be most of the nature
of happiness," unquote.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.810
If man is by nature in
relation to a divine good,

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:26.160
it must mean that he has
within himself some means

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.720
of coming to know it.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:33.670
With his finite mind, man can
somehow apprehend, at least

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:36.640
in part, the infinite.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.520
Though he is not
divine, man must

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:43.870
strive to be godlike through
the exercise of his reason.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:47.440
Aristotle counseled
that, quote, "We must not

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:53.410
follow those who advise us being
men to think of human things

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:57.910
and being mortal of
mortal things, but must,

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:03.280
so far as we can, make
ourselves immortal

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:07.810
and strain every nerve to live
in accordance with the best

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:09.430
thing in us.

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:13.300
For even if it be small
in bulk, much more

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:19.470
does it in power and worth
surpass everything," unquote.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:25.230
We see in Aristotle's
account an adumbration

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:29.430
of Thomas Aquinas's definition
of the final and perfect

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:33.170
happiness as,
quote, "nothing else

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:36.800
than the vision of the
divine essence," unquote.

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:41.480
Thus, it was, thanks
to Greek philosophy,

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:45.380
that reason became normative.

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:49.460
For the first time,
instead of dreams,

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:53.070
the entrails of
dismembered birds,

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.950
or signs in the
night sky, reason

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:59.830
came to be the arbiter
of right and wrong.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:02.790
It is through reason,
not from the gods

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:06.960
of the city, tribal mores,
or the spirits of things,

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.270
that man can
discern what is just

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.510
from what is unjust, what
is good from what is evil,

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:16.380
what is myth from
what is reality.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:22.980
He can now discern
natural causality

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.650
and describe to things
their true causes

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:30.000
rather than assigning magical
properties to everything.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.450
Red dog urine, contrary
to Babylonian belief,

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:37.110
is not actually a causal
factor in producing happiness.

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:44.190
Behaving reasonably or doing
what accords with reason

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:47.580
becomes the standard
of moral behavior

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.010
and therefore the
standard of law.

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:54.930
To do what is
unreasonable is wrong.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:58.180
As Aquinas reflecting
Aristotle would later say,

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:03.180
the essential character of sin
or vice is its irrationality.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.870
However-- I need
another drink of water.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.790
Aristotle's definition
of happiness

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:21.420
set up a major dilemma
of which we must be aware

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.880
if we are to understand
the significance

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:27.390
of the contribution
of Christianity.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:29.940
Both Socrates and
Aristotle admitted

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:34.750
that the attainment of wisdom
was so difficult that few,

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:38.290
if any, could achieve it.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:42.460
Aristotle said that leisure
is a necessary condition

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:45.520
for the contemplation
of the good.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:50.980
As only a few possess leisure,
this presents a huge problem.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:54.700
What's more, even
those with leisure

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:58.600
face nearly insurmountable
obstacles in obtaining wisdom

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:01.840
because human nature,
as Aristotle said,

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:07.840
is in many ways in bondage
so that, open quotes,

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:11.860
"the possession of wisdom
might be justly regarded

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:17.540
as beyond human power," unquote.

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:20.240
This statement seems to be
in tension with Aristotle's

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:24.040
teaching that nature
ever seeks an end,

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:27.850
and always the tendency in
each is towards the same end

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:30.880
and if there is no impediment.

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.750
How is it, then, that
in all of nature,

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:37.540
only man's end seems
to be practically

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:40.810
beyond the reach
because of seemingly

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:44.050
insurmountable impediments?

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:47.950
If the possession of wisdom is
the final perfection of man,

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:51.840
how is it that even
in the best regime,

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:55.000
the many will never be wise?

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.250
What about the
peasants, workers,

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:01.420
and slaves, who do not have
the possibility of reaching

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:03.880
the height of human attainment
because they have no leisure

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:05.740
to pursue it?

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:09.130
What is the purpose
or end of their lives,

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:13.810
only to support the leisure
of those who do pursue it?

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:20.640
Then most of humanity is left
out, doomed to frustration.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.720
Leo Strauss observed, quote,
"If striving for knowledge

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:28.560
of the eternal truth is
the ultimate end of man,

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.070
the man who is merely just
or moral, without being

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:36.200
a philosopher, appears as
a mutilated human being,"

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.660
unquote.

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:40.450
As Aquinas would
later say, quote,

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:45.450
"To strive for any end that
cannot be secured is futile,"

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:46.890
unquote.

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:49.560
Therefore, the happiness to
which all men are naturally

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:53.880
ordered as their final
perfection cannot consist

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:57.710
in the happiness
of only a very few.

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.560
That would indeed be futile.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.750
Thus, philosophy
raised a question

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:05.190
that it could not answer.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:07.070
How could man have
a nature whose end

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:09.950
is practically unobtainable?

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:15.810
The very keenness
of this conundrum

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:19.850
made Christian
revelation intelligible

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:24.200
to the Hellenistic world
when it first received it

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:28.280
because it addressed the problem
that man seemed to be made

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:32.530
for something beyond his reach.

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:35.350
Against the preceding Greek
philosophical background,

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:39.250
one can come closer to
realizing the electrifying

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:42.190
effect of the
opening of the Gospel

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:45.640
of John, which was
written in Greek.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:48.610
The English translation
uses "word" for "logos,"

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:51.730
but it can be rendered
just as well by "reason."

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:54.250
For our purposes here, let
us leave the Greek word

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:57.390
"logos" in the quotation.

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:03.070
Quote, "In the beginning
was the logos, and the logos

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:05.170
was with God.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:06.970
And the logos was God.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:12.560
He was in the
beginning with God.

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:16.550
All things were made
through him," unquote.

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:21.080
Revelation confirmed
Heraclitus's intuition

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:25.010
that logos makes the
universe comprehensible

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:30.660
because all things were
made by and through logos.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:36.320
Logos is not an it, an
intelligible principle,

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:40.730
or a cosmic force, but a who.

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:43.430
This is what John
announces so majestically,

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:45.770
the word incarnate.

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:49.730
The startling thing
about Christianity

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:52.490
is that logos does
not simply remain

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:58.850
as the wisdom behind the world,
but rather enters into it--

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:05.690
logos made flesh--
and dwelt among us.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:12.680
What if Heraclitus, having
speculated on logos,

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.020
met logos walking
through the door?

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:23.240
The face of logos made visible
is the transforming experience

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:28.070
of a Hellenized Christianity or
of a Christianized Hellenism.

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:30.500
The disclosure of
Christ as logos

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:33.560
could not be a clearer
message that revelation

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.560
involves reason.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:39.380
We also know that
creation was made

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:43.040
by way of his divine intellect.

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:46.800
God willed or loved
creation into being,

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:49.910
but it was and is
according to his word.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:52.400
At the level of
revelation, we now

00:27:52.400 --> 00:28:00.350
know why the world is rational,
because God is reason.

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:04.250
God speaks to man with equal
force through his reason

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:07.260
as he does through
divine revelation.

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:11.360
Recall Saint Paul's famous
words to the Romans.

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.620
Quote, "For since the
creation of the world,

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.700
his invisible attributes
are clearly seen,

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:21.730
his everlasting power, and
also his divinity," unquote.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.440
And does not Saint Peter
urge that Christians,

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:27.260
quote, "be ready
always to satisfy

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:32.240
everyone that asked of you
a reason of the hope which

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:34.370
is in you," unquote?

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:38.240
Early Church father,
Clement of Alexandria,

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:40.940
insisted on the relevance
of reason to faith.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:44.660
Quote, "Philosophy,
therefore, was a preparation,

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:49.710
paving the way for him who has
perfected in Christ," unquote.

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:52.280
The mid-century second
epistle of Clement,

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:57.310
also known as the recognitions,
spelled out the necessity

00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:59.230
of reason to faith.

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.450
It's worth hearing at length.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:08.030
Open quotes, "Do not think that
we say that these things are

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:11.960
only to be received
by faith, but also

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:15.200
that they are to be
asserted by reason.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:18.440
For indeed, it is not safe to
commit these things to bear

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:21.290
faith without reason
since assuredly,

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:24.140
truth cannot be without reason.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:27.830
And therefore, he who has
received these things fortified

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:32.930
by reason can never lose them,
whereas he who receives them

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:37.010
without proofs by an assent
to a simple statement of them

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:39.860
can neither keep
them safely, nor is

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:44.570
certain if they are true,
because he too easily believes

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:47.540
also easily yields.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:50.630
But he who has sought reason
for those things which

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:53.750
he has believed and
received, as though bound

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:56.570
by chains of reason
itself, can never

00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:59.780
be torn away or separated
from those things which

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:01.460
he hath believed.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:04.520
And therefore,
according as any one

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:08.210
is more anxious in demanding
a reason, by so much

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:12.390
will he be the firmer in
preserving his faith."

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:12.890
Excuse me.

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:22.430
Heaven forbid, Saint Augustine
would later exclaim, quote,

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:27.920
"that we should believe in such
a way as not to accept or seek

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:33.320
reasons since we could not even
believe if we did not possess

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:35.780
rational souls," unquote.

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:40.820
He also said, quote,
"Believers are also thinkers.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:46.220
In believing, they think, and
in thinking, they believe.

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:50.000
If faith does not think,
it is nothing," unquote.

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:57.640
Now, if God is logos,
reason and revelation

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:00.160
are not at an impasse.

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:02.530
A division of
labor defines them.

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:06.570
The first Vatican Council
put the distinction clearly.

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:11.380
There is a two-fold order
of knowledge distinct both

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:17.110
in origin and in object, in
origin because our knowledge

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:21.670
in the one is by natural reason
and in the other by divine

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:26.500
faith, in object because,
besides those things to which

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:31.120
natural reason can attain,
there are proposed to our belief

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:34.840
mysteries hidden in God
which, unless revealed,

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:37.360
cannot be known.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:40.480
The common objective is the
search for and the apprehension

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:43.870
of the highest things.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:47.590
In Christianity,
Father James Schall

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:55.510
said, quote, "Revelation
itself has turned to philosophy

00:31:55.510 --> 00:32:02.920
precisely to explain more fully
what is revealed," unquote.

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:08.710
Christian revelation confirms
reason in its authority.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:12.560
At the same time, revelation
has a claim on reason.

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:15.490
A philosophy that
a priori excludes

00:32:15.490 --> 00:32:20.030
the possibility of
revelation is a philosophy

00:32:20.030 --> 00:32:23.610
that is not true to itself.

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:29.010
On its own terms, philosophy
must remain open to revelation.

00:32:31.932 --> 00:32:35.360
Father Schall maintained that
revelation, though it discloses

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:38.000
truths beyond the
ability of philosophy

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.480
to arrive at
unassisted, nonetheless

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:45.020
addresses itself
to the same reason

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:46.850
that philosophy considers.

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:55.610
He said revelation articulates
a more clear and defined end

00:32:55.610 --> 00:33:00.170
than even the philosopher could
envision by his own powers,

00:33:00.170 --> 00:33:04.160
yet the efforts
of the philosopher

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:07.160
to envision this
end were themselves

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:12.920
needed to understand why
revelational answers were

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:16.940
answers to real questions
that did not arise only

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:20.140
from revelation.

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:22.390
Though he had an
inkling of it, Socrates

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:26.320
did not know that there is a
greater gift than philosophy,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:28.420
but without the
philosophical endeavor,

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.310
could man have
come to understand

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:34.630
the overwhelming significance
of this greater gift?

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:36.760
If the puzzle of
man's purpose had not

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:39.760
been posed by Aristotle,
could man have adequately

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:42.940
appreciated the solution to it?

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:45.010
If you haven't
asked the question,

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:49.280
you won't understand the answer.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:51.890
As mentioned earlier,
Aristotle saw the need

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:55.910
to, so far as we can,
make ourselves immortal

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:58.850
and strain every nerve to live
in accordance with the thing

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:00.710
that is best in us.

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:03.260
Yet he clearly perceived
that only a few with leisure

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:06.210
could even attempt it.

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:12.199
What of the vast
majority of mankind

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:14.889
who are incapable of
the philosophical life?

00:34:14.889 --> 00:34:20.780
Christianity solved this dilemma
posed by Aristotle by offering,

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:22.850
as Father Schall
noted, to encompass

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:28.489
a destiny that might include
all men, even nonphilosophers.

00:34:28.489 --> 00:34:30.770
This conclusion meant
that the highest things

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:33.350
were intended for more
than just philosophers.

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:37.909
Indeed, resurrection
accomplishes

00:34:37.909 --> 00:34:41.510
that final personal
happiness, which

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:45.960
political theory
initially proposes for man

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:49.620
but for which it had no answer.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.710
What Aristotle
could only vaguely

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:53.929
intimate regarding
man's striving

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:58.070
to imitate the divine comes to
startling fruition in Christ's

00:34:58.070 --> 00:35:03.260
offer to share his divine
life with each person.

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:07.070
Individual man moves from
paltry insignificance

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:10.340
to inestimable worth.

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:13.340
In the eyes of the world, man
is seen as next to nothing

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:16.370
in the brief span of his life.

00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:19.400
Christianity reveals otherwise.

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:25.640
For the first time, the hidden
identity of man is disclosed.

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:29.990
His natural end is superseded by
a supernatural one in which he

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:31.940
partakes of divine life.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.300
Saint John made the
extraordinary statement, quote,

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:40.070
"It does not yet appear
what we shall be,

00:35:40.070 --> 00:35:46.150
but we know that when he
appears, we shall be like him.

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:49.480
For we shall see him
as he is," unquote.

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:53.770
The implication is very clear
that man's destiny is actually

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:56.840
to share in God's divine life.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:36:01.230
Man's end, then,
is outside history

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:05.370
in personal union with the
transcendent and loving God,

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:08.160
something man could
not dare to have

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:13.740
hoped for, much less imagined,
until God revealed himself

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:16.830
in Christ as
desiring nothing more

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:19.590
than to share his inner
life with his creatures,

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:23.610
to impart to them
his own divinity.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:27.090
Each person could participate
in the divine order

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:32.590
of salvation as an individual,
not through his participation

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:36.900
as a citizen of his
polis or the mediation

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:39.240
of a semi-divine ruler.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.600
To say that this was a
revolutionary disclosure

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:46.180
is an understatement.

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:48.640
However, the harmonization
of faith and reason

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:52.930
in Christianity was disrupted
in the late Middle Ages

00:36:52.930 --> 00:36:55.310
by, among others,
William of Ockham

00:36:55.310 --> 00:36:59.400
through his exaggerated
concern for God's omnipotence

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:03.220
and absolute freedom of
will and his detestation

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:06.220
of what he thought was the
contamination of Christianity

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:10.690
by pagan, meaning
Aristotelian, metaphysics.

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:12.970
He challenged the
Thomist teaching

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:17.620
that God's will proceeds from
his divine intellect and not

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:19.550
the other way around.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:25.280
This is the core issue
of that time and our own.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:28.790
Aquinas argued that since
God is logos or reason

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:34.280
itself, his, quote, "will
follows upon his intellect,"

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:35.030
unquote.

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:37.100
Reason rules.

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:39.980
Will follows.

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.770
The word precedes action.

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:45.110
This was not a new
teaching in Christianity.

00:37:45.110 --> 00:37:50.060
Going back to the third century,
Hippolytus said of God, quote,

00:37:50.060 --> 00:37:56.600
"He thought of it, the cosmos,
willed it, spoke the word,

00:37:56.600 --> 00:37:57.790
and so made it," unquote.

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:03.930
The primacy of
intellect is clear.

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:06.870
The intellect directs the will.

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:10.170
The will then acts in
accord with reason.

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:12.300
Ockham thought that
God's freedom was

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:14.100
compromised by this teaching.

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:18.570
He flipped the relationship
between intellect and will.

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:22.950
God's will now becomes primary,
his intellect subordinate to it

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:25.260
as a mere instrument.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.630
This changes everything.

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:31.550
It is no longer God's knowledge
that constitutes being.

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:35.070
It's his will that does so.

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:41.290
In fact, God knows
because he wills.

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.060
Will precedes knowledge.

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:46.690
It is the act that
produces knowledge,

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:50.590
not knowledge that
produces the act.

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:54.550
Will becomes the
ontological principle.

00:38:54.550 --> 00:38:55.960
Let's be clear.

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.740
Either reason rules
and the will follows,

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:03.220
or will rules and
reason follows.

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:05.110
In the latter
case, reason simply

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:09.280
becomes a tool for the will
to use in however it wishes

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:11.740
to accomplish what it wills.

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:15.620
What's more-- and here
is the key, key point.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:19.030
It's impossible
intelligently to distinguish

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:25.720
among instances of will on
the basis of will alone.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:30.400
This was a revolutionary change
with seismic consequences.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:35.380
Unless the divine intellect is
precedent to the divine will,

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:39.130
logos cannot be imprinted
in creation as its essential

00:39:39.130 --> 00:39:40.540
aspect.

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:42.520
The more inscrutable
the divine will,

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:47.970
the more opaque reality
becomes as its product.

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:51.200
As Spanish scholar,
Fernández-Santamaría, wrote,

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:54.520
quote, "Ockham has done
away with the logos,

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:58.270
and all that is
left in God is will,

00:39:58.270 --> 00:40:02.620
a will that cannot be bound
or limited by reason-inspired

00:40:02.620 --> 00:40:07.130
actions or assumptions of man."

00:40:07.130 --> 00:40:11.510
Having expelled from the mind
of God the intelligible world

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:16.070
of Plato, French philosopher
Étienne Gilson said,

00:40:16.070 --> 00:40:19.730
Ockham was satisfied that no
intelligibility can be found

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:22.780
in any of God's works.

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:26.020
How could there be order in
nature when there is no nature?

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:31.130
Creation is not
imprinted with reason.

00:40:31.130 --> 00:40:34.770
It cannot reflect what
is not there as a result,

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:38.120
there is no rational order
invested in the universe upon

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:42.470
which one can rely, only the
second to second manifestation

00:40:42.470 --> 00:40:47.840
of God's will, which he can
change at any moment for no

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:48.340
reason.

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:55.100
Aquinas said that, quote, "The
first cause of the universe

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:59.840
is mind, and that the
last end of the universe

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:04.820
must be the good of
the mind that is truth,

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:06.830
and that in the
contemplation of truth,

00:41:06.830 --> 00:41:11.360
man finds the principal
object of wisdom," unquote.

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:15.140
For voluntarists like Ockham,
the first cause of the universe

00:41:15.140 --> 00:41:17.930
is will, not mind.

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:20.870
Therefore, the last end of the
universe cannot be the good

00:41:20.870 --> 00:41:22.820
of the mind.

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:25.640
The overthrow of the
relationship between reason

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:27.860
and faith is complete.

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:32.840
Man's final end is
no longer rational.

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:36.650
Without an inherent end by
which to judge the goodness

00:41:36.650 --> 00:41:40.310
or evil of his actions as
taking him toward or away

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:45.040
from the perfection of
his nature, man is adrift.

00:41:45.040 --> 00:41:49.600
He has no natural good
to guide him in reality.

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:51.940
According to
nominalist metaphysics,

00:41:51.940 --> 00:41:55.570
acts are intrinsically neither
good or bad except in so far

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.570
as God makes them so.

00:41:58.570 --> 00:42:02.380
As if in answer to Socrates's
his famous question

00:42:02.380 --> 00:42:06.340
in the Euthyphro,
voluntarists like Ockham

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:09.670
say God does not command certain
behavior because it is good.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:13.150
It's good because
he commands it,

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:17.170
and he can change his
will at any moment.

00:42:17.170 --> 00:42:19.270
There is not anything
objective in the character

00:42:19.270 --> 00:42:22.000
of acts themselves.

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:25.630
By his command, God could
turn vices into virtues

00:42:25.630 --> 00:42:27.880
and, for instance,
require us to commit

00:42:27.880 --> 00:42:32.670
adultery or even idolatry.

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:35.460
In this radical view
of God's omnipotence,

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:40.110
he is not only the primary cause
of things but the only cause.

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:44.220
There are no secondary causes
such as cause and effect

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:47.190
in the natural world.

00:42:47.190 --> 00:42:50.580
Gravity does not make
objects fall to the Earth.

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:52.410
God does.

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:55.170
Fire does not burn cotton.

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:57.740
God does.

00:42:57.740 --> 00:43:00.350
One must reject
the natural order

00:43:00.350 --> 00:43:06.200
of the universe to protect
God's omnipotence in this view.

00:43:06.200 --> 00:43:09.350
God was not to be limited
by any laws of nature's.

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:13.970
Essences are an affront to him.

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:21.620
This loss of intelligibility
provided, some centuries later,

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:27.980
the foundation for David Hume's
famous is-ought distinction

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:30.980
made in the 18th
century or what later

00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:34.910
came to be called the
fact-value distinction, which

00:43:34.910 --> 00:43:37.130
continues to afflict us today.

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:45.410
This asserts that there
can be no moral guidance

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:50.960
from what exists or what
is to what ought to be.

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:57.450
One cannot derive an ought from
an is or a value from a fact.

00:43:57.450 --> 00:44:00.350
This is another consequence of
claiming that nature cannot be

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:02.810
normative because there
are no ends in it,

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:06.620
no telos to guide things to
become what they should be

00:44:06.620 --> 00:44:10.400
in their fullest according
to their essences.

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:14.120
In short, there is no
foundation in reality

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:16.970
for what ought to be.

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:22.980
Once one is rid of essences,
there is not an ought in sight.

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:26.580
We are simply left with what is.

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:31.110
Machiavelli would take
great advantage of this.

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:36.210
It is no surprise that
Hume concluded that, quote,

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:42.360
"Reason is and ought only to
be the slave of the passions

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:45.600
and can never pretend
to any other office

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:50.520
than to serve and
obey them," unquote.

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:54.630
Your reason is a slave
to your passions.

00:44:54.630 --> 00:45:00.120
Well, it is no great leap from
the voluntarist exaltation

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:03.480
of the will of God to the
voluntarist exaltation

00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:05.160
to the will of man.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:09.120
If God is of a voluntarist,
we become voluntarists.

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:13.110
By "voluntarist," I mean
premised only on will, not

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:14.910
in reason.

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:19.110
After all, if God's intellect
is subordinate to his will,

00:45:19.110 --> 00:45:21.600
why should man be any
different in subordinating

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:24.390
his reason to his will?

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:29.490
As a consequence, one gets
to make up one's own values.

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:32.490
Truth is located in the will.

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:35.220
This becomes a very
dangerous teaching

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:37.770
when it is politicized.

00:45:37.770 --> 00:45:41.710
If law is no longer
reason, what is it?

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:44.830
Man's law becomes its
own standard, based only

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:48.820
on the will of the ruler,
whether one or many,

00:45:48.820 --> 00:45:53.320
predisposed to
nothing but itself.

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:56.050
As Bertrand de
Jouvenel warned, quote,

00:45:56.050 --> 00:45:59.470
"The man who finds in
God, before all else,

00:45:59.470 --> 00:46:03.040
will and power will be
disposed to the same view

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:05.380
of human government."

00:46:05.380 --> 00:46:10.390
40 years ago, German
philosopher Hans Blumenberg

00:46:10.390 --> 00:46:14.490
remarked, quote, "When God
becomes nominalism's Deus

00:46:14.490 --> 00:46:18.400
absconditus," the
hidden god, the god

00:46:18.400 --> 00:46:22.060
of whom we can know nothing
because he's pure will,

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:24.790
"the world loses
its rational base,

00:46:24.790 --> 00:46:27.670
and man is left to find
whatever meaning and purpose

00:46:27.670 --> 00:46:30.850
he can through his own efforts.

00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:35.620
And that leads to the modern
project of self-affirmation

00:46:35.620 --> 00:46:39.620
and self-assertion," unquote.

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:43.360
An early manifestation
of this self-assertion

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:48.490
came in the form of Marquis de
Condorcet's book, The Progress

00:46:48.490 --> 00:46:51.550
of the Human Mind, which
epitomizes the ambition

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:52.495
of the modern project.

00:46:55.120 --> 00:46:59.170
Quote, "My work will prove
by fact and reasoning

00:46:59.170 --> 00:47:02.440
that no bounds have been
fixed to the improvement

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:06.580
of the human faculties, that
the perfectability of man

00:47:06.580 --> 00:47:11.170
is truly limitless," unquote.

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:15.490
The phrase "the limitless
perfectability of man"

00:47:15.490 --> 00:47:19.720
captures the metaphysical
incoherence of modernity.

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:23.560
Limitless perfectability
is an oxymoron.

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:25.840
Anything limitless
is by definition

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:28.417
incapable of perfection.

00:47:28.417 --> 00:47:30.250
As we have seen, the
perfection of something

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:32.670
is the fulfillment
of its nature.

00:47:32.670 --> 00:47:36.580
The perfectability is without
limits, as Condorcet says.

00:47:36.580 --> 00:47:40.450
It means that it loses its
direction because nature

00:47:40.450 --> 00:47:44.800
is precisely about limits,
and if no direction exists,

00:47:44.800 --> 00:47:51.130
regression is indistinguishable
from progression.

00:47:51.130 --> 00:47:54.190
Alexis de Tocqueville wrote
that men of the revolution,

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:58.030
the French Revolution, quote,
"had a robust faith in man's

00:47:58.030 --> 00:48:00.490
perfectability and power.

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:03.010
They had no doubt that
they were appointed

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:06.040
to transform the human race.

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:09.760
These sentiments and
passions had become a sort

00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:13.360
of new religion," unquote.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:15.430
The object of faith
in this new religion

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:21.400
changes from God to humanity,
from a God-centered perspective

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:25.990
to one of man's self-sufficiency
or rather his self-creation,

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:31.110
but never to man as
he is, only to man

00:48:31.110 --> 00:48:34.700
as he could be or must become.

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:39.990
In the communist
version, Homo sapiens

00:48:39.990 --> 00:48:43.920
becomes Homo sovieticus.

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:46.290
Under the Nazi
ideology, man as he is

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:49.310
must be purged of
his racial impurities

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:55.620
to reign as ubermensch, the
over-man or the beyond-man,

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:59.520
who creates and
imposes his own values.

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:03.270
All of this testifies to the
truth of GK Chesterton's quip,

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:08.070
here paraphrased, that the man
who ceases to believe in God

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:10.380
does not believe in nothing.

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:13.680
Rather, he will
believe in anything.

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:18.240
Chesterton also said, quote,
"Just as one generation

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.900
could prevent the very
existence of the next generation

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:26.250
by all entering a monastery
or jumping into the sea,

00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:28.770
so one set of thinkers
can in some degree

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:30.990
prevent further
thinking by teaching

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:33.930
the next generation
that there's no validity

00:49:33.930 --> 00:49:37.320
in any human thought," unquote.

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:40.470
We seem now to have reached
that point of ignorance

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:44.070
because we have had more than
one set of thinkers espousing

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:50.510
this teaching for many and
for more than one generation.

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:55.730
If we don't know what
we are or who we are,

00:49:55.730 --> 00:49:59.550
we can self-identify
as anything.

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:03.050
This self-identification is not
the result of knowing oneself

00:50:03.050 --> 00:50:05.660
but of willing oneself.

00:50:05.660 --> 00:50:08.480
If what one wills is the
principal constituent

00:50:08.480 --> 00:50:14.170
of reality, there is no standard
by which one act of the will

00:50:14.170 --> 00:50:17.140
can be differentiated from
any other act of the will.

00:50:17.140 --> 00:50:20.830
Thus, I have no right
to judge your values,

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:23.050
and you cannot judge mine.

00:50:23.050 --> 00:50:25.660
The common good is the casualty.

00:50:25.660 --> 00:50:30.040
Therefore, a man can
self-identify as a woman.

00:50:30.040 --> 00:50:32.200
However, why can't
I self-identify

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:37.150
as someone who finds
that utterly absurd if I

00:50:37.150 --> 00:50:40.120
don't want to be canceled?

00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:42.940
We are living in a period
of moral and cultural

00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:45.940
self-asphyxiation.

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:51.430
It will end us if we don't
return to reality soon.

00:50:51.430 --> 00:50:54.820
Let's transpose the
self identity talk

00:50:54.820 --> 00:50:59.140
to an earlier period to
illustrate this absurdity.

00:50:59.140 --> 00:51:03.070
The Nazis, who believed
in the primacy of will,

00:51:03.070 --> 00:51:07.840
self-identified as racist
haters of Jews and Slavs, whom

00:51:07.840 --> 00:51:11.590
they proceeded to
slaughter and enslave.

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:16.030
Did opposition to this arise
because the opponent simply

00:51:16.030 --> 00:51:20.269
self-identified as anti-Nazis?

00:51:20.269 --> 00:51:27.520
No, it arose among those who
held that all men are created

00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:33.130
equal is a universal truth
and that therefore, what

00:51:33.130 --> 00:51:35.710
the Nazis were doing
was objectively evil

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.110
and must be stopped.

00:51:38.110 --> 00:51:42.070
The uncle after whom I
was named gave his life

00:51:42.070 --> 00:51:43.720
in order to stop it.

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:47.080
He did not make
that sacrifice so

00:51:47.080 --> 00:51:51.190
that people could
self-identify, but so

00:51:51.190 --> 00:51:54.370
that the universal truth that
is the true basis of freedom

00:51:54.370 --> 00:51:57.260
would be upheld.

00:51:57.260 --> 00:52:02.310
Well, what goes first,
faith or reason?

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:04.350
That's very much a
chicken-and-egg kind

00:52:04.350 --> 00:52:06.300
of question.

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:10.200
It seems to me that they
both stand or fall together.

00:52:10.200 --> 00:52:12.360
Without faith, reason
has shown itself

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:15.410
capable of reasoning
itself out of existence.

00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:24.110
Fulton Sheen once
described an intellectual

00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:29.210
as someone who's been educated
beyond his intelligence.

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:31.970
Reason without faith
ultimately ends in nihilism.

00:52:31.970 --> 00:52:37.100
Without reason, faith becomes
something like Sunni Islam.

00:52:37.100 --> 00:52:41.180
Today, there is not
a faith-reason fight.

00:52:41.180 --> 00:52:45.490
There is a faith-reason flight.

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:47.470
Our current
condition illustrates

00:52:47.470 --> 00:52:51.650
that a society will decline to
the extent that it loses faith,

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:55.610
and that the loss of reason
is concurrent with it.

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:59.260
In Democracy in America, Alexis
de Tocqueville observed, quote,

00:52:59.260 --> 00:53:04.240
"Anglo-American civilization
is the result of two distinct

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:08.350
elements, which in other
places have been in frequent

00:53:08.350 --> 00:53:13.000
disagreement, but which the
Americans have succeeded

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:16.690
in incorporating to some
extent one with the other

00:53:16.690 --> 00:53:19.300
and combining admirably.

00:53:19.300 --> 00:53:21.430
I allude to the
spirit of religion

00:53:21.430 --> 00:53:23.565
and the spirit of
liberty," unquote.

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:30.280
Can it be any surprise that
as our spirit of religion

00:53:30.280 --> 00:53:33.640
has declined, so, too,
has our spirit of liberty?

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:41.210
So we come full circle, if at
times by a circuitous route,

00:53:41.210 --> 00:53:42.920
to the question of
the relationship

00:53:42.920 --> 00:53:45.240
of faith and reason.

00:53:45.240 --> 00:53:48.660
And if you're told that your
faith excludes your reason,

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:51.430
ask how it knows that.

00:53:51.430 --> 00:53:54.020
If you're told your
reason exclude your faith,

00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:57.070
ask how it could know that.

00:53:57.070 --> 00:53:59.740
You need your reason to
understand your faith more

00:53:59.740 --> 00:54:03.250
fully, and you need your
faith to open your reason

00:54:03.250 --> 00:54:06.700
to what is otherwise beyond it.

00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:12.250
Always keep before yourself
your ultimate destination.

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:14.860
Seek the truth.

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:18.450
Recognize and know it.

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:20.220
Live by it.

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:22.950
Abide in it.

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:26.150
That is why we are here.

00:54:26.150 --> 00:54:29.600
This is our purpose.

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:32.210
God bless Hillsdale College.

00:54:32.210 --> 00:54:35.078
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:43.220 --> 00:54:44.840
MARLEIGH KERR: Thank
you, Mr. Reilly.

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:48.680
Mr. Reilly has agreed to sign
copies of his book, America

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:51.380
on Trial-- a Defense
of the Founding,

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:53.720
on the second floor of the
[INAUDIBLE] Lobby following

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:55.190
this session.

00:54:55.190 --> 00:54:57.693
We do have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:54:57.693 --> 00:55:00.110
please make your way to one
of the microphones in the side

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:00.610
aisles.

00:55:06.910 --> 00:55:09.533
AUDIENCE: You mentioned
the faith-fact-value split,

00:55:09.533 --> 00:55:11.200
which is something
we discuss, actually,

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:14.440
quite a bit in the Western
theological tradition core

00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:16.360
class, and then
right at the end,

00:55:16.360 --> 00:55:19.810
to live and abide by our faith.

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:21.970
Do you have any
suggestions on how

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:25.180
to open up that
conversation with people

00:55:25.180 --> 00:55:30.130
who are unwilling to listen
and how to bring that idea back

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:31.060
into our society?

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:35.350
ROBERT R. REILLY:
Well, I mean, this

00:55:35.350 --> 00:55:38.530
sounds like a perverse
comment, but thank

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:40.090
heavens for the Nazis.

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:42.220
I mean, you can
always say to them,

00:55:42.220 --> 00:55:45.010
ought we to have
opposed the Nazis?

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:46.030
Ought we to have?

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:51.510
Is that a fact, or
is that a value?

00:55:51.510 --> 00:55:56.170
Or how would you know
you ought to do that?

00:55:56.170 --> 00:55:59.550
There must be some moral
foundation for that.

00:55:59.550 --> 00:56:00.550
And the other thing is--

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:01.795
I find this very curious.

00:56:04.420 --> 00:56:14.180
No one goes to an eye doctor
and receives a diagnosis

00:56:14.180 --> 00:56:20.893
and then says to the doctor,
oh, well, that's what you think.

00:56:20.893 --> 00:56:22.560
Or if it's something
serious, maybe they

00:56:22.560 --> 00:56:25.490
get a second opinion, but they'd
never say, well, that's just--

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:29.830
I don't think that.

00:56:29.830 --> 00:56:32.440
That's just your opinion.

00:56:32.440 --> 00:56:34.930
No, why not?

00:56:34.930 --> 00:56:38.820
Well, because the
ophthalmologist

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:42.960
or the optometrist knows
what the eye is for.

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:43.620
What's it for?

00:56:43.620 --> 00:56:47.610
Well, to see.

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:51.500
And if it's not seeing, it's
suffering from a corruption

00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:52.670
and imperfection.

00:56:52.670 --> 00:56:56.230
And from his studies,
he would know

00:56:56.230 --> 00:57:00.950
wherein that imperfection lies
and may be able to correct it.

00:57:00.950 --> 00:57:02.240
That's why you go to him.

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:04.310
The only reason he
knows that is because he

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:09.345
knows the purpose of an eye,
what an eye ought to be.

00:57:09.345 --> 00:57:10.970
And we can march
through the human body

00:57:10.970 --> 00:57:15.340
and make the same observations.

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:17.200
Except when you get
below the waist,

00:57:17.200 --> 00:57:20.350
people say, well, what
could that possibly be for?

00:57:20.350 --> 00:57:22.660
Well, let's just experiment
and find-- no, no,

00:57:22.660 --> 00:57:24.970
we know just as
much about that as--

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:28.210
the natural moral law rules
that just as much as it

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:31.600
does everything else.

00:57:31.600 --> 00:57:39.050
So as people remonstrate
with you, they're saying,

00:57:39.050 --> 00:57:41.030
you ought not to.

00:57:41.030 --> 00:57:43.470
Well, that's an ought.

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:46.140
Where did they get it?

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:47.790
Probably from an is somewhere.

00:57:47.790 --> 00:57:52.170
They just don't understand that
the fact-value distinction is

00:57:52.170 --> 00:57:54.915
itself a corruption of reason.

00:57:58.795 --> 00:58:00.170
MARLEIGH KERR: We
have time for--

00:58:00.170 --> 00:58:00.860
ROBERT R. REILLY:
I'm sure that's

00:58:00.860 --> 00:58:02.640
going to win you
a lot of friends.

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:05.190
Just take them
right through that.

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:07.428
MARLEIGH KERR: We have
time for one more question.

00:58:07.428 --> 00:58:09.470
ROBERT R. REILLY: I'm
sorry for going on so long,

00:58:09.470 --> 00:58:11.150
but actually, I cut this talk.

00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:14.045
It would have been in more pain.

00:58:16.570 --> 00:58:17.078
Sir.

00:58:17.078 --> 00:58:17.620
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:58:17.620 --> 00:58:19.190
Thanks for being here.

00:58:19.190 --> 00:58:23.110
My question was about
discerning what the ultimate end

00:58:23.110 --> 00:58:23.920
of something is.

00:58:23.920 --> 00:58:27.280
You spoke well from
Aristotle that you determine

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:29.660
what's good based off
of something's end

00:58:29.660 --> 00:58:32.260
and to what degree it reaches
that end and to what degree

00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:35.080
it corrupts before
it gets to that end.

00:58:35.080 --> 00:58:37.270
It seems to me that many
reasonable people disagree

00:58:37.270 --> 00:58:43.600
about the end of man, and with
respect to many social issues,

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:45.790
also with respect
to political things,

00:58:45.790 --> 00:58:48.460
reasonable people disagree
about what the purpose of man is

00:58:48.460 --> 00:58:52.840
and what a good life looks like.

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:55.960
So what does that
bring us to, and how

00:58:55.960 --> 00:58:58.520
do we resolve those
reasonable disagreements?

00:58:58.520 --> 00:59:02.165
And what does faith have to
say in those disagreements?

00:59:02.165 --> 00:59:04.040
ROBERT R. REILLY: Well,
I think what you have

00:59:04.040 --> 00:59:07.000
and what you encounter
today more often than not

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:09.850
in such disagreements
is you're talking

00:59:09.850 --> 00:59:14.860
to someone who doesn't think
man has any purpose at all.

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:18.400
And therefore, it's
just, he says, she says,

00:59:18.400 --> 00:59:19.720
or that's your opinion.

00:59:19.720 --> 00:59:22.600
Or you don't have the
right to judge me,

00:59:22.600 --> 00:59:27.400
and there's no
basis for judgment,

00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:31.030
whereas they would admit
that those ends exist

00:59:31.030 --> 00:59:34.042
every time they go to a doctor.

00:59:34.042 --> 00:59:35.500
And yet all of a
sudden, they don't

00:59:35.500 --> 00:59:38.560
exist when it comes to
a moral issue, which

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:40.840
could be corrupting their soul.

00:59:40.840 --> 00:59:46.150
And so do you think anyone would
contest that the end of man

00:59:46.150 --> 00:59:49.560
is happiness?

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:51.990
Will they say it's unhappiness?

00:59:51.990 --> 00:59:55.620
Now, I don't think you'd
find many people who

00:59:55.620 --> 01:00:00.490
wouldn't say that the end of
our nature is to be happy.

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:02.700
They might say we don't
have a nature, just

01:00:02.700 --> 01:00:06.690
as people say that the
universe is accidental, right?

01:00:06.690 --> 01:00:08.820
How could anything have
a purpose if all of this

01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:13.410
happened by accident,
which is the alternative?

01:00:13.410 --> 01:00:17.250
If there is no logos, then
everything is accidental,

01:00:17.250 --> 01:00:18.870
and then of course,
there are no ends.

01:00:18.870 --> 01:00:20.340
There are just accidents.

01:00:23.020 --> 01:00:27.040
Now, if you can establish
that that end exists,

01:00:27.040 --> 01:00:31.530
like happiness, obtainable
through the life of virtue,

01:00:31.530 --> 01:00:35.850
there can be then quite
legitimate and very interesting

01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:38.600
arguments about the
means to reach that end.

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:43.580
And often, those
discussions are sort of

01:00:43.580 --> 01:00:49.430
on prudential matters, how best
to reach the acknowledged end

01:00:49.430 --> 01:00:53.840
of that happiness, which
is the ultimate perfection

01:00:53.840 --> 01:00:56.153
of our nature.

01:00:56.153 --> 01:00:57.820
The other thing you
know you'll find out

01:00:57.820 --> 01:01:02.185
is that there's something
wrong with death.

01:01:07.140 --> 01:01:11.460
No one wants-- well, very few
people really want to die,

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:14.720
and we rebel at the
thought of dying.

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:17.433
We rebel at the thought
of not existing.

01:01:17.433 --> 01:01:19.850
I remember when my kids were
very young, and one of them--

01:01:19.850 --> 01:01:23.090
I told them, where was
I when that picture was

01:01:23.090 --> 01:01:24.290
taken of the other kids?

01:01:24.290 --> 01:01:28.220
Well, Matthew, you
didn't exist yet.

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:30.380
I had to be.

01:01:30.380 --> 01:01:32.280
Well, no, actually, you didn't.

01:01:32.280 --> 01:01:34.350
You came into being.

01:01:34.350 --> 01:01:40.030
So once you have an
experience of death

01:01:40.030 --> 01:01:41.560
in a family or a
loved one, there's

01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:44.830
an enormous grievance there.

01:01:44.830 --> 01:01:49.490
You know there's something
wrong with death.

01:01:49.490 --> 01:01:53.290
Happiness must also
involve immortality,

01:01:53.290 --> 01:01:59.000
and that, of course, is what
Socrates argues for, mainly

01:01:59.000 --> 01:02:03.350
from justice, that we have to
have immortal souls in order

01:02:03.350 --> 01:02:08.780
for perfect justice to be
achieved in the afterlife.

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:11.090
But it also obtains to
the end of happiness.

01:02:11.090 --> 01:02:12.950
So there are all kinds
of human experience

01:02:12.950 --> 01:02:16.487
that scream out for
this kind of treatment.

01:02:20.070 --> 01:02:23.860
No, I guess that didn't--

01:02:23.860 --> 01:02:24.980
it's a tough question.

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:26.660
That's a really good question.

01:02:26.660 --> 01:02:28.570
Thank you for it.

01:02:28.570 --> 01:02:34.630
So I'm happy to talk to anybody
upstairs for the book signing.

01:02:34.630 --> 01:02:36.370
If you buy one, I thank you.

01:02:36.370 --> 01:02:39.820
My children thank you.

01:02:39.820 --> 01:02:42.070
Maybe I can even get
Larry Arnn to thank you.

01:02:42.070 --> 01:02:43.510
That would be [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:44.830
So thank you very much.

01:02:44.830 --> 01:02:47.880
[APPLAUSE]

